2.text:
On the real locus the involution $q\mapsto -q$ splits the Gauss-Manin
system of

\begin{equation*}
{\cal C}_{E}:y^{2}=2E-q^{2}+q^{n},\qquad \omega_{E}=dq/y ,
\end{equation*}
into its $+$ and $-$ eigenspaces.  On the $+$ eigenspace

\begin{equation*}
u_{r}(2m)=m-1=\frac n2-1,\qquad 
u_{r}(2m+1)=2m=n-1,
\end{equation*}
so that on the real locus

\begin{equation*}
u_{r}(n)=\frac n2-1\iff n\equiv0\pmod 2 ,\qquad 
u_{r}(n)=n-1\iff n\equiv1\pmod 2 ,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
u_{r}(n)=\frac n2-1,\qquad u_{r}(n\pm1)=n\pm1-1
\end{equation*}
holds for every even $n>0$.  Up to the free particle $n=2$ the first
non trivial even exponents are therefore

\begin{equation*}
n_{1}=4,\qquad n_{2}=6,
\end{equation*}
and with the degenerate convention $n_{1}^{0}=2,\;n_{2}^{0}=4$.
Put $m=n_{1}/2$.  On the $+$ eigenspace the real period of

\begin{equation*}
H_{m,\mu}(p,q)=\frac12\Bigl(p^{2}+q^{2}-\mu q^{m}\Bigr)
\end{equation*}
can be written

\begin{equation*}
T_{m}(\mu ,E)=\sqrt{2E}\;{\cal T}_{m}\!\Bigl(\mu(2E)^{(m-2)/2}\Bigr),
\qquad {\cal T}_{m}(0)=2\pi ,
\end{equation*}
and ${\cal T}_{m}$ is the ordinary real hypergeometric period.  For
the pair $n_{1}=4,\;n_{2}=6$

\begin{equation*}
m_{1}=\frac{n_{1}}2=2,\qquad 
\mu_{0}=\frac{2}{n_{1}}\sqrt{\frac{n_{2}-n_{1}}{n_{1}/2}}
      =\frac{2}{4}\sqrt{\frac{2}{2}}=\frac12,
\end{equation*}
and up to an inessential additive constant

\begin{equation*}
H_{0}(p,q)=\frac12\Bigl(p^{2}+q^{2}-\mu_{0}q^{2}\Bigr)
        =\frac12p^{2}+\frac{1-\mu_{0}}2q^{2}
        =\frac12p^{2}+\frac14q^{2},
\end{equation*}
i.e. the ordinary harmonic oscillator
$\omega_{0}^{2}=1-\mu_{0}=1/2$, $\omega_{0}=2^{-1/2}$.  For $m=2$

\begin{equation*}
T_{2}(\mu ,E)=\oint\frac{dq}
{\sqrt{2E-(1-\mu)q^{2}}}=\frac{2\pi}{\sqrt{\,1-\mu\,}},
\end{equation*}
so that the hypergeometric factor is $E$--independent.  In the
conventional $ {\cal T}_{m}(0)=2\pi$ normalisation

\begin{equation*}
{\cal T}_{2}(\mu)=\frac{2\pi}{\sqrt{1-\mu}},\qquad 
{\cal T}_{2}(0)=2\pi,
\end{equation*}
and with the normalised ${\cal T}_{m}^{0}={\cal T}_{m}/2\pi$,
${\cal T}_{m}^{0}(0)=1$,

\begin{equation*}
{\cal T}_{2}^{0}(\mu)\equiv1,\qquad0\le\mu<1,
\end{equation*}
the familiar constant $A_{3}\!-\!A_{5}$ real period.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\alpha_{0}=\frac{n_{1}-1}{n_{2}}=\frac{4-1}{6}=\frac12,
\qquad {\cal T}_{2}^{0}(\alpha_{0})=1,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the physical $T(0)=2\pi$ normalisation

\begin{equation*}
T_{2}(E)\equiv\frac{2\pi}{\omega_{0}}=2\pi\sqrt2,\qquad 
T_{2}^{h}=\frac{\pi}{\omega_{0}}=\pi\sqrt2,
\end{equation*}
the constant full and half periods of the oscillator
$H_{0}=\tfrac12(p^{2}+\omega_{0}^{2}q^{2})$.
With the usual ${\cal T}_{m}^{0}(0)=1$ hypergeometric normalisation
this is the constant normalised value

\[
{\cal T}_{2}\!\left(\frac{n_{1}-1}{n_{2}}\right)=1,
\]

the $2\pi\sqrt2$ statement being the $T(0)=2\pi$ form of the same
constant.  In the degenerate free-particle convention
$n_{1}^{0}=2,\;n_{2}^{0}=4$, $\alpha_{0}^{0}=1/4$, $\mu_{0}=1$,
$\omega_{0}=1$ and $T_{1}^{0}\equiv2\pi$, i.e. ${\cal T}_{1}^{0}\equiv1$,
the ordinary $2\pi$ harmonic period, which is the $n_{1}=4,n_{2}=6$
limit above.  This is the constant harmonic limit of the $A_{n-1}$
family.  The $n_{1}=2,n_{2}=4$ free limit is $T\equiv2\pi$, i.e.
${\cal T}\equiv1$, in the same normalisation. ∎

