5.text:
On the $q\mapsto -q$ $+$ eigenspace

\begin{equation*}
{\cal C}_{E}:y^{2}=2E-q^{2}+q^{n},\qquad 
\omega_{E}=dq/y ,
\end{equation*}
the real Gauss-Manin rank is

\begin{equation*}
u_{r}(2m)=m-1=\frac n2-1,\qquad 
u_{r}(2m+1)=2m=n-1 ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. on the real locus $u_{r}(n)=n/2-1\iff n\equiv0\pmod 2$ and
$u_{r}(n)=n-1\iff n\equiv1\pmod 2$.  Hence

\begin{equation*}
u_{r}(n)=\frac n2-1 ,\qquad u_{r}(n\pm1)=n\pm1-1
\end{equation*}
is satisfied for every even $n\ge2$.  Modulo the free particle
degeneration $n=2$ the first non-trivial even exponent is $n=4$ and the
next admissible even exponent is $n=6$.  With the degenerate free
convention $n_{1}^{0}=2$, $n_{2}^{0}=4$ is the literal first pair.  In
the usual non–free convention

\begin{equation*}
n_{1}=4,\qquad n_{2}=6,\qquad m_{1}=\frac{n_{1}}2=2,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\mu_{0}
      =\frac{2}{n_{1}}\sqrt{\frac{n_{2}-n_{1}}{n_{1}/2}}
      =\frac12 .
\end{equation*}
Up to an additive constant

\begin{equation*}
H_{0}(p,q)=\frac12\Bigl(p^{2}+q^{2}-\mu_{0}q^{m_{1}}\Bigr)
        =\frac12p^{2}+\frac{1-\mu_{0}}2q^{2}
        =\frac12p^{2}+\frac14q^{2},
\end{equation*}
i.e. the ordinary harmonic oscillator $\omega_{0}^{2}=1-\mu_{0}=1/2$.
On the $+$ eigenspace

\begin{equation*}
T_{m}(\mu ,E)=\sqrt{2E}\;{\cal T}_{m}\!\Bigl(\mu(2E)^{(m-2)/2}\Bigr),
\qquad {\cal T}_{m}(0)=2\pi ,
\end{equation*}
and for $m=2$

\begin{equation*}
T_{2}(\mu ,E)=\oint\frac{dq}{\sqrt{2E-(1-\mu)q^{2}}}
           =\frac{2\pi}{\sqrt{1-\mu}},\qquad
{\cal T}_{2}(\mu)=\frac{2\pi}{\sqrt{1-\mu}} .
\end{equation*}
The hypergeometric factor is $E$–independent.  In the normalised
${\cal T}_{m}^{0}={\cal T}_{m}/2\pi$, ${\cal T}_{m}^{0}(0)=1$,
convention

\begin{equation*}
{\cal T}_{2}^{0}(\mu)\equiv1,\qquad0\le\mu<1,
\end{equation*}
the familiar constant $A_{3}\!-\!A_{5}$ harmonic limit.  Now

\begin{equation*}
\alpha_{1}=\frac{n_{1}-1}{n_{2}}=\frac{4-1}{6}=\frac12,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
{\cal T}_{2}^{0}(\alpha_{1})=1,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the physical $T_{m}(0)=2\pi$ normalisation

\begin{equation*}
T_{2}(E)\equiv\frac{2\pi}{\omega_{0}}
        =\frac{2\pi}{\sqrt{1-\mu_{0}}}=2\pi\sqrt2,
\qquad T_{2}^{h}=\frac{\pi}{\omega_{0}}=\pi\sqrt2,
\end{equation*}
the constant full and half periods of the oscillator
$H_{0}=\tfrac12\bigl(p^{2}+\omega_{0}^{2}q^{2}\bigr)$.  In particular
the normalised real hypergeometric period is the constant

${\cal T}_{2}^{0}\bigl((n_{1}-1)/n_{2}\bigr)=1$,

the $2\pi$–normalised form being $T_{2}\bigl((n_{1}-1)/n_{2}\bigr)=
2\pi/\omega_{0}=2\pi\sqrt2$.  With the degenerate free-particle
convention $n_{1}^{0}=2$, $n_{2}^{0}=4$, $\alpha_{0}=1/4$,
$\mu_{0}=\sqrt2$, after the translation $q\mapsto q-1/\sqrt2$,
$H_{0}=\tfrac12\bigl(p^{2}+q^{2}\bigr)+{\rm const}$,
$\omega_{0}=1$ and $T_{1}^{0}\equiv2\pi$, i.e. ${\cal T}_{1}^{0}\equiv1$,
$T_{1}(1/4)=2\pi$ physically and $1$ in the ${\cal T}_{m}^{0}(0)=1$
normalisation.  This is the literal $n_{1}=2$, $n_{2}=4$ free limit
of the same constant harmonic statement. ∎

3.text:
