convention $n_{1}^{0}=2,\;n_{2}^{0}=4$ one obtains
$\alpha_{0}=1/4$, $\mu_{0}=1$, $\omega_{0}=1$ and
$T_{1}(E)\equiv2\pi$, ${\cal T}_{1}^{0}\equiv1$.  This is the constant
harmonic limit of the $A_{n-1}$ family.  In the beta normalisation

\[
T_{m}(\alpha)=\frac{2\pi^{3/2}}{\Gamma(\alpha)\Gamma(\tfrac12+\alpha)}
\]

the $m=2$ limit is $T(1/2)=2\pi$, i.e. the normalised value $1$.
Up to the inessential $2\pi$ factor this is the constant
$A_{3}\!-\!A_{5}$ real period quoted in the literature.

Remark.  Discarding the $m=2$ harmonic collapse the first genuine
$m\ge3$ case is $m_{1}=3$, i.e. $n_{1}=6$.  With the usual
non-degenerate choice $n_{2}=2n_{1}=12$ one has
$\alpha=(n_{1}-1)/n_{2}=5/12$ and on the $+$ eigenspace

\[
T(\alpha)=\frac{2\pi^{3/2}}{\Gamma(\alpha)\Gamma(\tfrac12+\alpha)},
\qquad 
T\!\left(\frac5{12}\right)=
\frac{2\pi^{3/2}}{\Gamma(5/12)\Gamma(11/12)},
\]

the $2\pi$ normalised full real period; the half-period normalised
form is $\pi^{3/2}/\!\bigl[\Gamma(5/12)\Gamma(11/12)\bigr]$.  This is
the non-trivial hypergeometric limit of the family. ∎

